## 6. Request → response data flow

<!--
One canonical flow: a typical read request, then a typical write request.
Each step lists the file/function and what crosses the boundary.
-->

### 6.1 Read flow (typical)

```text
client
  └── HTTPS request ──▶ edge (TLS, rate-limit)
        └── HTTP ──▶ API process (gunicorn worker)
              └── router match ──▶ controller (FastAPI route)
                    └── auth check ──▶ middleware/guard
                          └── service.use_case (business logic)
                                └── repository.find (ORM)
                                      └── SQL ──▶ database
        ◀── response (JSON, possibly cached) ──┘
```

| Step | File / function | Boundary crossing |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `<edge.conf:line>` | TLS, rate limit |
| 2 | `<router.match>` | URL → handler |
| 3 | `<auth.middleware>` | token/session verify |
| 4 | `<controller.handler>` | request validation |
| 5 | `<service.use_case>` | business rules |
| 6 | `<repository.find>` | ORM call |
| 7 | `<db.execute>` | SQL over wire |

### 6.2 Write flow (typical)

```text
client
  └── write request ──▶ controller
        └── validate (pydantic / zod / ...)
              └── service (business rule check + transaction begin)
                    ├── repository.create (INSERT)
                    ├── repository.update (UPDATE)
                    ├── cache.invalidate
                    ├── event.publish (outbox pattern?)
                    └── transaction.commit
        ◀── 201 Created + new resource ──┘
```

| Step | File / function | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `<controller>` | DTO validation |
| 2 | `<service>` | Transaction boundary |
| 3 | `<repository>` | SQL execution |
| 4 | `<cache>` | Invalidation strategy |
| 5 | `<event bus>` | Sync/async, retry |

